-*-Indented-Text-*-

              Dylan in Common Lisp

This is the Common Lisp implementation of the language described in
"Dylan(TM) an object-oriented dynamic language" (Apple Computer Eastern
Research and Technology, April 1992): Dylan compiled to Common Lisp as it
is entered, at a read-eval-print loop or a file at a time.  (The other
implementation in this tree, a compiler from Dylan to C, is in ../compiler
and ../runtime; see ../README.md.)

It began as a direct translation of Thomas -- the Dylan(TM)-compatible
compiler and runtime that Digital Equipment Corporation's Cambridge
Research Lab wrote in Scheme against that book -- into Common Lisp.  Since
then it has been corrected against the manual, chapter by chapter, and
rebuilt for speed: generic dispatch through caches (generic.lisp), direct
calls, and inline arithmetic, element and iteration with guards
(runtime-fast-paths.lisp).  Every file still says which file of Thomas it
grew from, and the correspondence is tabulated below; the notes after it
describe how the Scheme became Common Lisp, which still shapes the code.

This directory replaced Thomas's own src/, portable/ and kits/
directories, which have been removed, and took the name src/ from the
first of them.  The three transcripts those kits produced are kept in
reference/, as the baseline of the regression test.  ../README.thomas is
the 1992 Thomas README, and still describes the Scheme directory layout as
it stood then.

It is developed and tested on SBCL.  Nothing in it is SBCL-specific except
weak hash tables and weak pointers (SB-EXT) and one SB-INT query for whether
a symbol is a special variable, all in compat.lisp with portable fallbacks.
load-dylan.lisp compiles the files into fasl/<lisp>-<version>/ the first
time, and afterwards recompiles only a changed file and those after it.

                             RUNNING IT

    $ sbcl
    * (load "load-dylan.lisp")
    * (dylan:rep)

    Entering Dylan read-eval-print-loop.
    Exit by typing "dylan:done"

    ? (define-method double ((n <number>)) (+ n n))

    ;Value: double

    ? (double 21)

    ;Value: 42

    ? dylan:done

There are three ways to use it, as in the original:

  a) Compiler.  Load "load-compiler.lisp".  This provides

        (DYLAN:COMPILE-TO-FILE <file-name> . expressions) compiles the Dylan
      EXPRESSIONS and writes the resulting Lisp program to <FILE-NAME>.

        (DYLAN:COMPILE-FILE input output) compiles the INPUT file of Dylan
      expressions into the OUTPUT file.  (DYLAN:COMPILE-FILE shadows the
      Common Lisp symbol of that name.)

      The expressions COMPILE-TO-FILE is handed must be Dylan forms, whose
      symbols are interned in the DYLAN package -- what DYLAN::DYLAN-READ
      produces.  COMPILE-FILE reads its input that way for you.

  b) Runtime execution.  Load "load-runtime.lisp".  The output of the
     Dylan compiler can then be LOADed and run.

  c) Interactive environment.  Load "load-dylan.lisp".  This gives you

        (DYLAN:REP) starts a read-eval-print loop that uses a Dylan
      evaluator instead of a Lisp one.  Errors your Dylan code does not
      trap reach the Lisp debugger.

        (DYLAN:EMPTY-ENVIRONMENT!) forgets the module variables created by
      previous DYLAN:REP interactions.

To run the regression test, load "full-test.lisp" and call
(DYLAN:TEST-EXAMPLES); it reads ../dylan-examples.dyl and prints a
transcript to compare against reference/MIT.output.  See reference/README.

                          FILE CORRESPONDENCE

The names in the right-hand column are Thomas's Scheme files -- the ones
each file was first translated from.  They are paths within Thomas's own tree,
not within this one, and they no longer exist here.

  package.lisp             new -- the DYLAN package
  globals.lisp             new -- special proclamations for the runtime's
                             global variables, which nearly every file
                             refers to before its definer is loaded
  compat.lisp              portable/*.scm and kits/MIT/mit-specific.scm:
                             populations, weak tables, escape continuations,
                             numbers, sorting, the Dylan reader and printer,
                             and the condition-system interface
  support.lisp             src/support.scm
  comp-util.lisp           src/comp-util.scm
  comp-module.lisp         new -- modules (design notes 32 and 33)
  compiler.lisp            src/compiler.scm
  comp-method.lisp         src/comp-method.scm
  comp-class.lisp          src/comp-class.scm
  comp-sf.lisp             src/comp-sf.scm
  comp-exc.lisp            src/comp-exc.scm
  class.lisp               src/class.scm
  generic.lisp             src/generic.scm
  class-structure.lisp     src/class-structure.scm
  runtime-*.lisp           src/runtime-*.scm
  runtime-extensions.lisp  the language extensions the MIT kit added
  runtime-fast-paths.lisp  new -- guards for the inline arithmetic and
                             element calls the compiler emits
  rep.lisp                 src/portable-rep.scm
  full-test.lisp           full-test.scm
  load-dylan.lisp          src/load-*.scm and the kits' loaders
  load-compiler.lisp       src/load-compiler.scm
  load-runtime.lisp        src/load-runtime.scm

                        HOW THE TRANSLATION WORKS

Naming.  A colon cannot appear in a Common Lisp symbol without escaping, so
the two prefixes the Scheme used became:

    dylan:X    ->  dylan/X    names visible to compiled Dylan code.  These
                             are VARIABLES holding Dylan-callable
                             procedures, which take the two extra leading
                             arguments (multiple-values and next-method).
    dylan::X   ->  dylan%X    names used by the runtime library and by the
                             compiler's output, but not available from
                             Dylan.  Ordinary Lisp calling conventions.

Dylan's division operator therefore lives in the variable DYLAN// and its
/= in DYLAN//=.  Likewise implementation-specific:X became
implementation-specific/X, and the internal compiler temporaries that were
spelled !HANDLER-CASE:EXIT and the like are now !HANDLER-CASE/EXIT.

Truth.  Thomas was developed on MIT CScheme 7.2, in which #F and '() are
the same object.  The manual makes them distinct -- #f is the only false
value, and the empty list is true (chapters 3 and 8) -- and so are they
here.  Dylan lists are Lisp lists, so the empty list is Common Lisp's NIL;
#f is an object of its own, the value of DYLAN%FALSE, and #t is T.  A
Dylan value is therefore tested with TRUE? or FALSE?, never with Lisp's own
IF, and a Lisp truth value handed back to Dylan goes through BOOL (or
OR-FALSE, for "the object, or #f for none").  The runtime iterates through
INITIAL-STATE, NEXT-STATE and the other helpers in
runtime-collections-iterate.lisp, which hand it NIL for the protocol's #f;
a state of () is carried as +EMPTY-LIST-STATE+ and given back to Dylan as
().  Compiled code that contains a
literal #f is written to a file as #.dylan::dylan%false, which reads back
as the same object.

Records.  Every record type is statically known, so
portable/record.scm is gone and the six record types are DEFSTRUCTs with
the accessor names the rest of the system already used (slot.getter,
class.slots, instance.data, and so on).  Update these with SETF rather than
the old set-...! procedures.

Continuations.  CALL-WITH-CURRENT-CONTINUATION is used only to escape
-- BIND-EXIT, HANDLER-CASE, MEMBER? and the test harness -- so it became a
CATCH/THROW pair, and DYNAMIC-WIND became UNWIND-PROTECT.

Conditions.  The Scheme reflected the host Scheme's condition system into
Dylan's.  Here the host is Common Lisp: DYLAN-CONDITION carries a message
and a list of irritants, IMPLEMENTATION-SPECIFIC/CATCH-ALL-ERRORS is a
HANDLER-BIND, and a Dylan handler that declines simply returns, so the
remaining Lisp handlers run.

Reading.  Dylan programs are read with the host reader.  Common Lisp's own
reader cannot do the job: a colon is a package marker whose constituent
trait no readtable may change, and Dylan writes its keywords with a
trailing colon (foo:); nor does Common Lisp know #t and #f.  compat.lisp
therefore contains a small reader for Dylan's surface syntax.  A Dylan
keyword foo: is read as the Common Lisp keyword :FOO, and KEYWORD? is
KEYWORDP; every other identifier is interned in the DYLAN package with its
case folded up, except that the symbols t and nil get lower-case names, so
as not to be Common Lisp's T and NIL.  The spelling a symbol or keyword was
first entered in is remembered and used to print it (chapter 15); a
compiled file records the spellings of its symbols.  #key, #rest and #next are read as
the !key, !rest and !next the compiler uses; #x, #o, #b, #c(...), nested
#| ... |# comments and backquote (`, ",", ",@") are read as in Common
Lisp.  Chapter 3 defers backquote to a separate section the manual does
not contain, so it follows the usual Lisp semantics, nesting included.

Printing.  For the same reasons there is a printer of its own: NIL prints
as (), T as #t, :FOO as foo:, floats as the manual shows them (9.0,
0.5), and a simple condition or type error as its message (chapter 13).  PP pretty-prints Dylan; LISP-PP pretty-prints the compiler's
Lisp output.

Module variables.  In the Scheme a Dylan module variable and a Dylan
lexical variable of the same name both compiled to the same Scheme symbol,
and the lexical one shadowed the global.  A Common Lisp global has to be
proclaimed special, which would have turned every like-named lexical
binding into a dynamic one, so module variable X is emitted as !MODULE/X/
and the compiler makes the shadowing explicit (see REALLY-COMPILE in
compiler.lisp and NAME->MODULE-VARIABLE in comp-util.lisp).

Modules.  Thomas had a single namespace.  comp-module.lisp adds the
modules of chapter 5 with the DEFINE-MODULE form of design note 32 (in
prefix form) and the module: file header of design note 33.  A name is
resolved at compile time: VARIABLE->NAME answers the Lisp name of the
variable the name refers to in the current module, and the compiler works
with Lisp names as before.  DYLAN owns the predefined variables; DYLAN-USER
uses DYLAN, and its own variables keep their earlier names (!MODULE/X/);
a variable X owned by module M is !MODULE/X@M/.

Generated code.  BEGIN became PROGN, SET! became SETQ, dotted lambda lists
became &REST, and LETREC became LET plus SETQ.  Scheme's CASE became a COND
over MEMBER: it compares with EQV? -- Common Lisp's EQL -- and Common Lisp's
CASE would read a clause whose key list is (t) or (otherwise) as the
default.  Compiled Dylan binds more variables than it always uses, so
IMPLEMENTATION-SPECIFIC/EVAL muffles the host compiler's style warnings.

                    DIFFERENCES FROM THE SCHEME VERSION

The regression suite (../dylan-examples.dyl) produces the same values as
reference/MIT.output except for these.  The first five are where the manual
and Scheme disagree, and the manual is followed:

  * #f and () are different objects, and #f prints as #f.  MIT Scheme
    printed both as (), and took (whatitis #f) for the empty list.

  * Floats print as the manual's examples print them: 9.0 and 0.5 where MIT
    Scheme wrote 9. and .5.  Numbers read as in Common Lisp, so 3. is the
    integer 3.

  * SQRT is as defined in Common Lisp (chapter 14), so (sqrt 25) is 5.0,
    not Scheme's exact 5.

  * The book's <displaced-view> example (page 58) reads a slot that
    nothing initialized: the example's <view> slot has no init-keyword, so
    position: initializes nothing.  The manual makes that an error, and
    "uninitialized slot accessed" is signalled; Thomas, which did not keep
    a filtered slot's storage, answered (displace-transform ()).

  * MAP-INTO passes the function the elements of the source collections
    only, as chapter 12's prose and design note 27 say, so the chapter's
    example (map-into x + '(1 2 3 4)), with x = (100 100 200 200), answers
    (1 2 3 4).  The example and Thomas show (101 102 203 204), passing the
    target's elements too.

The rest ../README.thomas already describes as implementation-dependent:

  * PRINT and WRITE-LINE return their argument.  MIT CScheme returned an
    unspecified object and printed it as #[...]; Gambit and Scheme->C
    returned #F.  The three reference transcripts disagree with each other
    here.

  * Object printed representations (#[dylan-class <window>] and the like)
    of course differ, as the original output file notes.

  * The compiler's echo of the input wraps lines differently, because the
    line breaking is the host pretty printer's.

One limitation is new:

  * kits/MIT/restart-example.dyl did not run, and was removed with the rest
    of the kit: it is written against MIT Scheme's condition system by way
    of the SCHEME-PROCEDURE extension.  The Common Lisp equivalent of that
    extension is LISP-PROCEDURE, and the example would have to be rewritten
    against Common Lisp's conditions.

